## Claim
Text: "The U.S. surgeon general and the Centers for Disease Control both previously said that ‘masks are not effective in preventing [the] general public from catching coronavirus.’"
Claim author: Yinon Weiss
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://thefederalist.com/2020/10/29/these-12-graphs-show-mask-mandates-do-nothing-to-stop-covid/

## Initial Q&A
### Who held the position of U.S. Surgeon General during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020?
Answer: U.S. Surgeon General Dr. Jerome Adams held the position during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020. As the source notes, "U.S. Surgeon General Dr. Jerome Adams" was actively involved in pandemic response, and he himself acknowledged that "We were wrong back in February and March based on the fact that we didn't think there would be this high a degree of asymptomatic spread of coronavirus."

Source URL: https://www.fox5ny.com/news/im-begging-you-us-surgeon-general-pleads-with-americans-to-wear-face-masks-amid-virus-surge

### Did the U.S. Surgeon General in early 2020 publicly state that masks are not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus?
Answer: Yes. On March 8, 2020, in an interview with "Face the Nation," U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams stated that "masks do not work for the general public and preventing them from getting coronavirus." He later acknowledged this guidance needed to be "corrected" as the scientific understanding of the virus evolved.

Source URL: https://www.cbsnews.com/news/coronavirus-surgeon-general-jerome-adams-wearing-masks-face-the-nation/

### What was the exact wording of any statement made by the U.S. Surgeon General in 2020 regarding the effectiveness of masks for the general public against coronavirus?
Answer: In a March 2, 2020 interview on "Fox & Friends," U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams stated: "One of the things [the general public] shouldn't be doing is going out and buying masks... It has not been proven to be effective in preventing the spread of coronavirus amongst the general public... Folks who don't know how to wear them properly tend to touch their faces a lot, and actually can increase the spread of coronavirus." He also said in a March 31, 2020 "Fox & Friends" interview that "the data doesn't show" that wearing masks in public will help people, and in an April 3, 2020 White House press briefing he acknowledged that "the CDC, the World Health Organization, my office, and most public health and health organizations and professionals originally recommended against the general public wearing masks, because based on the best evidence available at the time, it was not deemed that that would have a significant impact on whether or not a healthy person wearing a mask would contract COVID-19." However, his recommendation changed by April 3, 2020, when he began recommending cloth face coverings for the general public in light of new evidence about asymptomatic transmission.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/

### On what specific date did the U.S. Surgeon General make a statement about masks not being effective for the general public?
Answer: According to the search result, U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams made the statement about masks not being effective for the general public on **March 8, 2020**, during an interview on "Face the Nation," where he said "masks do not work for the general public and preventing them from getting coronavirus."

Source URL: https://www.cbsnews.com/news/coronavirus-surgeon-general-jerome-adams-wearing-masks-face-the-nation/

### Did the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) or a CDC official publicly state in 2020 that masks are not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus?
Answer: The search result does not contain evidence that the CDC or a CDC official stated that "masks are not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus." On the contrary, the article describes the CDC actively correcting such interpretations, stating: "the interpretation that more mask-wearers are getting infected compared to non-mask wearers is incorrect," and clarifying that "At no time has CDC guidance suggested that masks were intended to protect the wearers." The article focuses on Trump misinterpreting a CDC study, not on the CDC itself making the claim in question.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/trump-repeats-inaccurate-claim-about-masks-citing-cdc-study-n1243562

### What was the exact wording of any statement made by the CDC or a CDC official in 2020 regarding the effectiveness of masks for the general public against coronavirus?
Answer: According to the search result, CDC spokesperson Arleen Porcell stated: "CDC does not recommend that people who are well wear a facemask to protect themselves from respiratory illnesses, including COVID-19." The article also notes that the CDC explained this by saying, "The science says that surgical masks won't stop the wearer from inhaling small airborne particles, which can cause infection. Nor do these masks form a snug seal around the face." Notably, the claim's exact phrasing about masks being "not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus" is attributed in this article to Surgeon General Jerome Adams's tweet, not the CDC itself.

Source URL: https://www.vox.com/2020/3/31/21198132/coronavirus-covid-face-masks-n95-respirator-ppe-shortage

### What was the stated context and reasoning behind the U.S. Surgeon General's and CDC's statements about mask effectiveness for the general public in early 2020?
Answer: In early 2020, U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams and the CDC recommended against the general public wearing masks based on the best available evidence at the time, which suggested masks would not significantly impact whether a healthy person would contract COVID-19. Key reasons cited included concerns that "folks who don't know how to wear them properly tend to touch their faces a lot, and actually can increase the spread of coronavirus," the risk of giving wearers "a false sense of security," and the critical need to "reserve PPE for the people who most need it" — namely healthcare workers facing N95 respirator shortages. However, on April 3, 2020, Adams reversed this guidance after receiving new CDC evidence showing that "a significant portion of individuals with coronavirus lack symptoms" and that asymptomatic and pre-symptomatic individuals could transmit the virus to others, leading the CDC to recommend cloth face coverings in public settings where social distancing was difficult to maintain.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/

### Did the U.S. Surgeon General and CDC later change their public guidance to recommend or require masks for the general public to prevent the spread of coronavirus?
Answer: Yes. According to the search result, both the U.S. Surgeon General and the CDC changed their guidance in early April 2020. On April 3, 2020, Surgeon General Jerome Adams announced in a White House press briefing that he had received new guidance from the CDC, stating: "In light of this new evidence, CDC recommends and the [COVID-19] task force recommends wearing cloth face coverings in public settings where other social-distancing measures are difficult to maintain," such as grocery stores and pharmacies. This marked a reversal from his earlier February and March 2020 statements advising the general public not to wear masks.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/

### Is the quoted phrase "masks are not effective in preventing [the] general public from catching coronavirus" a verbatim quote or a paraphrase of the original statements made by the U.S. Surgeon General and the CDC?
Answer: The quoted phrase is essentially a verbatim quote from U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams's February 29, 2020 tweet, with only minor modifications. Adams tweeted: "They are NOT effective in preventing general public from catching coronavirus," while the claim renders it as "masks are not effective in preventing [the] general public from catching coronavirus." The only changes are: replacing the pronoun "They" with "masks," changing "NOT" to lowercase "not," and inserting the article "[the]" before "general public." However, the claim's attribution to both the U.S. Surgeon General and the CDC is misleading—the quoted text is specifically from the Surgeon General, not from a CDC statement.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/04/02/coronavirus-us-surgeon-general-asks-cdc-to-see-if-face-masks-are-effective.html

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence clearly establishes that U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams tweeted on February 29, 2020 that masks are "NOT effective in preventing general public from catching coronavirus"—nearly a verbatim match to the claim's quoted phrase. The CDC's early 2020 guidance also conveyed a substantively similar position: the CDC did not recommend face masks for well people to protect themselves from COVID-19, and the agency's own explanation noted that "surgical masks won't stop the wearer from inhaling small airborne particles." Furthermore, Adams himself confirmed in an April 3, 2020 press briefing that "the CDC, the World Health Organization, my office... originally recommended against the general public wearing masks, because based on the best evidence available at the time, it was not deemed that that would have a significant impact on whether or not a healthy person wearing a mask would contract COVID-19." However, the evidence also explicitly notes that the exact quoted phrase in the claim is specifically attributed to the Surgeon General's tweet, not to a CDC statement, and the CDC used different wording to express its similar position.

## Information Still Needed

No further investigation is necessary. The evidence is sufficient to make a determination.

## Conclusion

The claim is technically accurate in substance—both the Surgeon General and the CDC held the position that masks were not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus in early 2020. However, the claim attributes a specific direct quote (in single quotation marks) to both entities, when the evidence explicitly shows that this exact phrasing came from the Surgeon General's tweet alone. The CDC held an aligned position but expressed it in different words. This constitutes a misleading presentation because the specific quoted phrase is being attributed to the CDC when only the Surgeon General used those exact words, creating a false impression that both said the same thing verbatim.

