### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is misleading in its attribution: the specific quoted phrase—"masks are not effective in preventing [the] general public from catching coronavirus"—is a near-verbatim reproduction of [U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams's February 29, 2020 tweet](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/04/02/coronavirus-us-surgeon-general-asks-cdc-to-see-if-face-masks-are-effective.html), not a CDC statement. While the CDC did hold a substantively aligned position in early 2020—[a spokesperson stating that "CDC does not recommend that people who are well wear a facemask to protect themselves from respiratory illnesses, including COVID-19"](https://www.vox.com/2020/3/31/21198132/coronavirus-covid-face-masks-n95-respirator-ppe-shortage)—the agency expressed this in different words, and Adams himself [confirmed in an April 3, 2020 press briefing](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/) that both his office and the CDC "originally recommended against the general public wearing masks" because it "was not deemed" to have a "significant impact" on whether a healthy person would contract COVID-19. By early April 2020, [both reversed their guidance](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/coronavirus-surgeon-general-jerome-adams-wearing-masks-face-the-nation/) as evidence emerged about asymptomatic transmission. The claim is therefore substantively accurate but presents a Surgeon General quote as if both he and the CDC said the same thing verbatim, constituting a misleading attribution.